Students will investigate the unique properties of nanomaterials for various purposes, including their use as contrast agents in cell imaging and therapeutics for cancer treatment. The curriculum covers five main disciplines of nanomedicine: analytical tools, nanoimaging, nanomaterials and nanodevices, novel therapeutics and drug delivery systems, and clinical, regulatory, and toxicological considerations. Graduates will be prepared to contribute to the growing field of nanomedicine and its applications in the pharmaceutical industry.

